{"author":{"id":"3d25152926154a0617ff34e11d8f9b1da058d0e4d697b5432f1ba8b4fe2c23f0"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791908699,"nonce":"WF5+3mHFvJXniRcAzL9TMPpWpIE=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOOEv6_s8AAAABbpQMnw","github-url":"https://github.com/graphcore-research/gwr/issues/422#issuecomment-6150163615"},"message":"This issue seems to be that a clock's tick count is only incremented when advanced, and the simulation engine only advances a clock when it has the next scheduled `Waker`\n\nSo, if a task attempts to wait some ticks of a clock which hasn't had a scheduled `Waker` in a while, then the clock's tick count will be out of date, and the new `Waker` will be scheduled for the wrong time (potentially in the past, as seen in the reproducer)\n\nNote that advancing a clock also handles any pending `Resolve`s on that clock. So, in the above scenario, the task could also access state which should have been updated via a `Resolve` at the end of some past tick, but hasn't because the `Resolve` is still pending\n\nThe current plan for addressing this:\n\n- For tick counts, enabling clocks to bring themselves up to date with simulation time, e.g. via shared state containing the current simulation time and phase\n\n- For `Resolve`s, reserve the final tick phase and schedule events when needed","files":null}]}